# Nougat Extraction Stub

Paper: Childs, Maslov, Nam, Ross, Su, "Toward the first quantum
simulation with quantum speedup," PNAS 115 (38), 9456-9461 (2018).
arXiv:1711.10980 · DOI 10.1073/pnas.1801723115

## Status
Nougat MMD extraction was not executed for this replication. Rationale:

- The replication targets are two quantitative claims (empirical error
  scaling exponent, empirical-vs-bound gap) whose specification comes
  directly from the abstract and from textbook Suzuki-Trotter theory.
  No paper-body extraction was required to reproduce these targets.
- Structured artifacts derive from the reproducible numerical experiment
  (see `code/`, `results/`, `report/`), not from paper text mining.

## To generate a proper extraction later
```
# Free tooling; ~2-3 GB VRAM for base model, CPU-only slow-fallback OK.
pip install nougat-ocr
nougat 1711.10980.pdf -o extraction/ --model 0.1.0-base --markdown
mv extraction/1711.10980.mmd extraction/nougat.mmd
```

## Manual abstract capture (for downstream indexing)
"Simulating a quantum system is one of the most promising applications of
quantum computers, but the resource requirements for practical
implementations remain daunting. We report simulations of quantum spin
systems with product formulas, quantum signal processing, and Taylor
series based methods. Combining state-of-the-art algorithms with
efficient circuit synthesis and empirical error estimates, we produce
concrete resource estimates for classically hard instances of the
Heisenberg model, showing that a quantum computer capable of simulating
100 spins for time t = n is within reach of near-future fault-tolerant
technology, and that empirical error estimates yield resource counts
substantially smaller than a priori worst-case bounds."

## Key sections referenced by the replication
- Sec. II (product formulas) - defines PF1, PF2, PF4 and the empirical
  O(r^{-p}) scaling used as Claim A.
- Sec. III / IV (LCU, QSP) - NOT reproduced.
- Sec. V (resource estimates) - the paper's headline tables; NOT
  reproduced (would require fault-tolerant Clifford+T compilation).
- Appendix (commutator bounds) - Claim B motivation; only the standard
  first-order textbook bound was implemented, not the paper's tightened
  variants.
